A||||9||WisconsinGT||Someone told me...||||1||
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||05-23-2000||09:41 PM||||that since I do not have a Cat. converter on my car, the O2 sensor would not work properly. Is this true? <P>Also, I bought a new coolent sensor. Can/do I need to put something on the threads before I put it in? Oh yeah, can someone give me the exact location for the coolent sensor on my 88GT 2.8? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phaeton||05-23-2000||11:41 PM||||The O2 sensor will still work.  It could probably be located so that it wouldn't work correctly, but that would take both planning and work, and it still might work some even then.<P>The coolant sensor has been working well for so long I cannot remember where it is.  That doesn't help you much but I think my Fiero spoils me.||||reg||12
Z||000002||theogre||05-24-2000||10:08 AM||||which coolant sensor.<P>ECM. use teflon pipe tape. the sensor does not ground thru the shell.<P>fan or gauge/idiot light. Never use teflon tape. use pipe dope. This sensor must have a good ground.<P>DO NOT over tighten them. they are NPT tapered thread. overtightening them can cause damage.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||05-24-2000||11:43 AM||||<BR>My O2 sensor is locacted in the exhaust manifold.  The Cat is located underneath the car after the O2 sensor.  I'm not sure how not having a cat would affect the operation of the O2 sensor.||||reg||5
Z||000004||My7Fieros||06-09-2000||02:39 AM||||Hi I just replaced my coolant sensor on my 88 V6. Look below the the thermostat housing..theres a "something" (sorry) directly under the housing. Its some sort of exhuast resending sensor thingy muh giggy..anyway, its got a screw under it. Take off that screw, and push that thing to the side. Then look behind where that was on the block and youll see the sensor. I guess basically what Im saying is, look on the pass. side of the block under the thermostat housing...(its late and Im tired, sorry for the confusion!)<BR>It takes a 19mm wrench with short turns because the dogbone gets in the way. <BR>Use plumbers teflon tape.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||06-09-2000||10:35 AM||||What's the sensor on the air filter can?  For those that have eliminated the air filter can (I just know I've seen at least one), what have you done with the sensor and does it matter?||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||06-09-2000||11:46 AM||||The sensor on the air filter can is the Intake Air Temperature sender. Sometimes called the Manifold Air Temperature sensor.<P>IAT or MAT. Same purpose. Provides the ECM with the temperature of the air entering the induction system.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||06-09-2000||02:14 PM||||Thanx.  How necessary is it?  What happens if it's either disconnected or mounted outside the box?  I thought about adding one of the K & N stand alone filters.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Fret Naught||06-09-2000||10:07 PM||||On my 86 SE V6 the o2 sensor is located ahead of the cat. I would not go so far as to say I didn't have a cat, but if I didn't I would bet it wouldn't effect the performace in a negative way at all. I might even be bold enough to expect my gas milage to increase. But then this is all hypotheotical.||||reg||1
Z||000009||terryk||06-09-2000||10:48 PM||||Monkeyman,<P>If you enjoy Code 23 you can disconnect it. It moves some spark and fuel around in open loop (cold engine) but nothing cosmic. I'd leave it hooked up, but you could mount it somewhere it can read the air temperature. <P>During really cold winters or super hot summers, it might make starting and warm up rough without it. Strap it to the K&N on the outside or mount it on the flat rubber end. <P>The IAT/MAT is not like the CTS (coolant). The CTS makes serious changes in fuel and spark. The IAT/MAT are far more subtle.||||reg||1
